{"id":"24478401-151c-4b3b-bba1-627f03664db5","audio_id":"5ec245c8-5ecd-4c9f-9a0f-59e2f80b9be4","status":"complete","created_at":"2026-11-16T04:58:46Z","answer":"The lead is a bright, thin, deliberately childlike synthetic-sounding voice, pitched/formant-shifted and tightly tuned, gated, and lightly reverberant. It mostly delivers short, two-pitch “cat/meow” and “dog/woof” calls in a staccato chant; there is some simple melodic contour, but little varied, flowing singing. The bouncy four-beat backing has a firm kick, crisp backbeat claps, and active synth bass. The verse-like repetitions and hook/breakdown differ mainly in density: around 0:30–0:37 the backing thins to claps and exposed vocals before the fuller groove returns. The key words sound intelligible; the clipped delivery around 0:33–0:36 is an audible rhythmic edit, not evidence of a missing lyric. The slightly artificial pitch glide on the drawn-out “meow” around 1:04–1:06 is also audible. No clear rushed or unintelligible words stand out. The longest apparent instrumental-only span is the short opening, roughly 0:00–0:02; the audio description also places the vocal entry at the opening, so that gap is uncertain rather than clearly a full two seconds. The ending has a final pitch-dropped “woof,” with the last vocal around 1:14. The beat tracker measures 118.2 BPM in 4/4; the groove is brisk and steady, and the earlier rough listening estimate was slightly higher. First vocal is at about 0:00; last vocal is about 1:14.

